* Hex grid, radius 7.

* Each player places 4–5 ships of known sizes (2, 3, 3, 4, 5 cells — same as classic), *and* secretly assigns each a **heading** (1 of 6) and **speed** (1 or 2 hex/turn).

* Each turn:

  <ol style="--ol-counter: cr-PyS5o3wrW6; counter-reset: var(--ol-counter) 0;">
  <li>
    <p>
      All defender's ships advance by their current speed.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Attacker fires N probes (e.g., 3).
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Hits are reported; hit cells are marked permanently as <b>footprints</b>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>

* Damage: each hit removes that ship's leading cell *and* drops its speed by 1. A ship at speed 0 is **adrift** (classic Battleship for that ship).

* Edge behavior: ships **bounce** off the board edge (heading reflects) — keeps them in play, but the bounce reveals info.

* Win: sink all opponent ships.

### 🌊 **Design B: "Patrol"**

Same as A, but ships travel **back and forth along a hidden line segment** instead of straight courses. Defender hides: the axis, the length of the patrol, and the phase. Damage shortens the patrol (slower = shorter line).

### 🎭 **Design C: "Convoy"**

Ships don't move independently — they move as a **formation**. One hidden "flagship" sets the heading; all other ships follow it. Killing the flagship strands the rest. *One* big hidden parameter instead of many small ones.

## The Crackpot Observation

The deepest thing about adding movement: **probing becomes an investment, not just an action.** In classic Battleship, every shot is an immediately-realized inquiry. With moving ships, shooting at cell X tells you "a ship is/isn't at X *right now*." Five turns later, that information has decayed by the speed of the ship.

So the strategic question shifts from *"where do I shoot?"* to *"where will the ship be when my shot lands?"* — which is **lead-the-target gameplay**. That's a fundamentally different cognitive task than classic Battleship, and it's the same skill a hunter, an anti-aircraft gunner, or a pool player uses. Universally intuitive.

## Recommendation Before We Visualize

Lock in two things and I'll render it:

<ol style="--ol-counter: cr-0hvjm8OeCx; counter-reset: var(--ol-counter) 0;">
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Design A, B, or C</b> as the baseline?
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Footprint mechanic</b> (hit cells stay marked forever) <b>yes or no</b>? This is the single biggest knob on "how imperfect is the information."
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>

My vote: **Design A + footprints YES**. The footprints give the attacker the breadcrumbs they need to infer trajectories, while the *current* position of every (still-living) ship remains hidden. That's the right tension.

